<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="60–70 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Collocations & word families: <em>retrieve the partnership and choose the right job</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Knowing one word is not enough for flexible use. You also need to know which words commonly travel with it, which preposition follows it, which family member fits the grammatical slot, how its meaning changes, where the stress falls, and whether the expression fits the setting. Learn <span class="ex">progress</span> as <span class="ex">make steady progress on a project</span>, then connect it to the verb <span class="ex">progress</span> and hear the stress change.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">60–70 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">verb · noun · adjective · adverb</span>
    <span class="tag">partnership · slot · meaning · register</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Compare grammatically possible wording with the partnerships speakers normally retrieve.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Map</b><span>Connect verbs, nouns, modifiers, prepositions, and family members through meaning and grammatical job.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Hear</b><span>Track stress and sound changes instead of predicting pronunciation from spelling alone.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Reuse</b><span>Build a vocabulary record and deliver a natural project briefing with flexible forms.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can produce high-value verb-noun, adjective-noun, adverb-adjective, adverb-verb, and preposition partnerships; distinguish a conventional partnership from a universal grammar rule; choose noun, verb, adjective, adverb, person, and process forms from a word family; recognize important spelling, meaning, and stress changes; select a natural alternative for a neutral, conversational, or formal setting; create a reusable vocabulary record; and describe a fictional project with accurate chunks and flexible word forms.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You recovered a familiar make-do contrast, an -ly adverb after a verb, and a work collocation that packages a completed time requirement.">
    <h3>Reactivate chunks, adverbs, and work language</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Collocation foundation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="make a decision and do the research">make a decision and do the research</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="explained the route slowly">explained the route slowly</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="met the Friday deadline">met the Friday deadline</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did a decision and made the research">did a decision and made the research</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Complete the neutral U.S. English contrast: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="make a decision and do the research" data-hint="Decision normally partners with make; research can partner with do in neutral use." data-fix="Make a decision and do the research.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The instructor spoke at a low speed so every learner could follow. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="explained the route slowly" data-hint="Use the -ly adverb to modify how she explained the route." data-fix="She explained the route slowly.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The team submitted the complete file by the required Friday time. It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="met the Friday deadline" data-hint="Meet a deadline packages successful completion by the required time." data-fix="The team met the Friday deadline.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice why correct grammar can still sound translated</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Westbridge project update: draft and edit</h3>
    <p><b>Translated draft:</b> <span class="ex">“We did important progress. Our apply was success, and we presented the results clear.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Natural edit:</b> <span class="ex">“We made significant progress. Our application was successful, and we presented the results clearly.”</span></p>
    <p><b>Later update:</b> <span class="ex">“After the successful application, the project progressed steadily. The team made a practical decision and acted decisively.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which verb normally partners with <span class="ex">progress</span> when progress is a noun?</li>
      <li>Which family member names the submitted document or request?</li>
      <li>Which family member describes the application after <span class="ex">was</span>?</li>
      <li>Which form modifies the verbs <span class="ex">presented</span>, <span class="ex">progressed</span>, and <span class="ex">acted</span>?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected make progress, the noun application, the adjective successful, and the adverb clearly from the grammatical and lexical context.">
    <h3>Discovery check: retrieve the partnership and fill the slot</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Westbridge edit choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="made significant progress">made significant progress</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="application">application</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="successful">successful</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="clearly">clearly</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did important progress">did important progress</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>For the conventional verb-noun partnership, the team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="made significant progress" data-hint="Retrieve make progress and use an adjective that naturally describes degree or importance." data-fix="The team made significant progress.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>After the possessive <span class="ex">our</span>, the sentence needs the noun <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="application" data-hint="A possessive determiner introduces the thing submitted, not the action form apply." data-fix="Our application was successful.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>After the linking verb <span class="ex">was</span>, the word describing the application is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="successful" data-hint="A linking verb connects the subject to an adjective description here." data-fix="The application was successful.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The form that modifies how the team presented the results is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="clearly" data-hint="Use an adverb to modify the action verb presented." data-fix="The team presented the results clearly.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: vocabulary is a network, not a single translation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A <b>collocation</b> is a combination that speakers use together often enough to retrieve as a partnership. Grammar may allow several combinations, but their naturalness, meaning, frequency, region, or register can differ. English commonly uses <span class="ex">heavy rain</span>, <span class="ex">strong evidence</span>, and <span class="ex">deeply concerned</span>. The alternatives <span class="ex">strong rain</span>, <span class="ex">powerful evidence</span>, and <span class="ex">deeply available</span> are grammatically shaped phrases, but they are not the conventional partnerships for those meanings.</p>
  <p>A <b>word family</b> connects forms through a core meaning, but the members do different jobs and can develop distinct meanings. <span class="ex">Apply</span> names an action; <span class="ex">application</span> can name a request, a form, or a practical use; <span class="ex">applicant</span> names a person. Do not assume that adding a suffix produces the form, spelling, pronunciation, or meaning you need.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>relationship</th><th>question to ask</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>verb + noun</td><td>What action normally happens to this noun?</td><td><span class="ex">make progress · reach an agreement · meet a deadline</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>adjective + noun</td><td>Which description conventionally fits this noun?</td><td><span class="ex">heavy traffic · strong evidence · practical solution</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>adverb + adjective or verb</td><td>Which kind or degree of modification sounds natural?</td><td><span class="ex">deeply concerned · highly effective · strongly recommend</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>word + preposition</td><td>Which relationship follows the word?</td><td><span class="ex">responsible for · apply for · depend on · respond to</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>family member</td><td>Which grammatical job and exact meaning does the slot require?</td><td><span class="ex">succeed · success · successful · successfully</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><b>Boundary:</b> A collocation is not a ban on every creative or less frequent combination. Writers can deliberately use unusual wording, and conventional choices can vary across communities and English varieties. This lesson prioritizes broadly natural current U.S. English and labels legitimate alternatives instead of pretending there is always only one possible phrase.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the verb-noun, adjective-noun, adverb-adjective, preposition, and word-family relationships from complete sentence frames.">
    <h3>Name the vocabulary relationship being tested</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Vocabulary relationship choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="verb plus noun">verb plus noun</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="adjective plus noun">adjective plus noun</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="adverb plus adjective">adverb plus adjective</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="word plus preposition">word plus preposition</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="word-family member selected by grammatical job">word-family member selected by grammatical job</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="two random words with no relationship">two random words with no relationship</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">The team reached an agreement</span>, the partnership <span class="ex">reached an agreement</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="verb plus noun" data-hint="Reached carries the action, and agreement is its noun object." data-fix="Reached an agreement is a verb-plus-noun collocation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">The report contains strong evidence</span>, <span class="ex">strong evidence</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="adjective plus noun" data-hint="Strong describes the quality of the noun evidence." data-fix="Strong evidence is an adjective-plus-noun collocation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">Residents were deeply concerned</span>, <span class="ex">deeply concerned</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="adverb plus adjective" data-hint="Deeply modifies the degree of the adjective concerned." data-fix="Deeply concerned is an adverb-plus-adjective collocation.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>In <span class="ex">Mara is responsible for the schedule</span>, <span class="ex">responsible for</span> is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="word plus preposition" data-hint="The adjective responsible conventionally takes for before the area of responsibility." data-fix="Responsible for is an adjective-plus-preposition partnership.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In <span class="ex">Their application was successful</span>, choosing <span class="ex">successful</span> after <span class="ex">was</span> is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="word-family member selected by grammatical job" data-hint="The linking-verb slot requires an adjective from the success family." data-fix="Successful is the word-family member selected for an adjective slot.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build verb-noun networks around a situation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>verb</th><th>high-value noun partners</th><th>reusable frame</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">make</span></td><td>progress, an effort, a decision, a mistake, a recommendation</td><td><span class="ex">We made steady progress on the report.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">do / conduct</span></td><td>research, an analysis, an experiment; work, homework</td><td><span class="ex">They did initial research. The team conducted a formal survey.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">take</span></td><td>action, responsibility, notes, a course, a risk</td><td><span class="ex">The coordinator took responsibility for the delay.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">reach / come to</span></td><td>an agreement, a conclusion, a decision</td><td><span class="ex">Both groups reached an agreement on the schedule.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">meet</span></td><td>a deadline, a requirement, a need, a target</td><td><span class="ex">The new schedule meets the evening group’s needs.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">raise</span></td><td>awareness, a concern, a question, funds</td><td><span class="ex">The survey raised an important question about access.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">gain</span></td><td>experience, confidence, access, support</td><td><span class="ex">Participants gained practical interview experience.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Learn the noun with more than one likely verb when the meaning changes. You can <span class="ex">make a decision</span>, <span class="ex">reach a decision</span>, <span class="ex">explain a decision</span>, <span class="ex">reverse a decision</span>, or <span class="ex">delay a decision</span>. The verbs express different stages. A useful vocabulary record stores the partnership and one complete frame, not a long unranked list.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched progress, a formal survey, responsibility, the deadline, and awareness with the action established by each project context.">
    <h3>Retrieve the complete partnership from the situation</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Verb-noun collocation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="made steady progress on the guide">made steady progress on the guide</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="conducted a formal survey of 80 residents">conducted a formal survey of 80 residents</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="took responsibility for correcting the file">took responsibility for correcting the file</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="met the June 15 deadline">met the June 15 deadline</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="raised awareness of the free workshops">raised awareness of the free workshops</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did steady progress to the guide">did steady progress to the guide</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The guide became more complete every week. The writing team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="made steady progress on the guide" data-hint="Retrieve make progress and use on before the project receiving the work." data-fix="The team made steady progress on the guide.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The methods section describes a planned questionnaire with 80 named participants and a published procedure. The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="conducted a formal survey of 80 residents" data-hint="Conduct is the more formal verb for carrying out the documented survey." data-fix="The team conducted a formal survey of 80 residents.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Nia acknowledged that she had uploaded the wrong file and personally organized the correction. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="took responsibility for correcting the file" data-hint="Take responsibility for is followed by a noun or -ing form naming the responsibility." data-fix="Nia took responsibility for correcting the file.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The completed application was submitted at noon on June 15, the final required day. The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="met the June 15 deadline" data-hint="Meet a deadline means complete the required action by the stated time." data-fix="The team met the June 15 deadline.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Before the campaign, few residents knew the workshops were free; afterward, 70% of surveyed residents knew. The campaign <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="raised awareness of the free workshops" data-hint="Raise awareness of names increased public knowledge of the supplied topic." data-fix="The campaign raised awareness of the free workshops.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Choose modifiers by semantic preference, not by intensity alone</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>partnership</th><th>examples</th><th>meaning relationship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>adjective + noun</td><td><span class="ex">heavy traffic/rain · strong evidence/argument · close relationship · major issue/change · practical solution · reliable source</span></td><td>the adjective conventionally describes that kind of noun</td></tr>
      <tr><td>adverb + adjective</td><td><span class="ex">deeply concerned · highly effective · fully aware · widely available · closely related</span></td><td>the adverb fits the quality or degree expressed by the adjective</td></tr>
      <tr><td>adverb + verb</td><td><span class="ex">strongly recommend · highly recommend · seriously consider · fully understand · closely examine</span></td><td>the adverb fits the stance or manner of the action</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Very</span> is often grammatically and naturally possible: <span class="ex">very effective, very concerned</span>. A more specific collocation can package a conventional degree or stance, but it is not automatically “better.” Choose <span class="ex">deeply concerned</span> when the concern is serious, <span class="ex">highly effective</span> when evaluation shows a strong degree of effectiveness, and <span class="ex">widely available</span> when something can be obtained in many places or by many people.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected heavy traffic, strong evidence, deeply concerned, widely available, and seriously consider from the supplied noun meaning and speaker purpose.">
    <h3>Choose the modifier that fits the exact noun or stance</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Modifier collocation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="heavy traffic">heavy traffic</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strong evidence">strong evidence</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="deeply concerned">deeply concerned</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="widely available">widely available</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="seriously consider">seriously consider</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="powerfully available">powerfully available</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The buses lose 25 minutes in dense rush-hour vehicle congestion. The schedule is affected by <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="heavy traffic" data-hint="Heavy is the conventional adjective for a large amount of traffic." data-fix="The schedule is affected by heavy traffic.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Three independent records directly support the same date and amount. The review has <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strong evidence" data-hint="Strong conventionally describes evidence with substantial support." data-fix="The review has strong evidence for the claim.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Residents describe the repeated unsafe crossing as serious and urgent. They are <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="deeply concerned" data-hint="Deeply packages a serious degree of concern." data-fix="Residents are deeply concerned about the crossing.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The guide can be downloaded free from every branch website and collected at all 24 libraries. It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="widely available" data-hint="Widely describes access across many locations or channels." data-fix="The guide is widely available.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The cost and access evidence is important enough for the board to evaluate the evening option carefully before deciding. The board should <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="seriously consider" data-hint="Seriously consider expresses careful attention to an important possibility." data-fix="The board should seriously consider the evening option.">choose</button> it.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read the grammatical slot before choosing the family member</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>slot clue</th><th>likely job</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>determiner or possessive + ___</td><td>noun</td><td><span class="ex">the decision · her application · their success</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>subject + tense marker + ___</td><td>verb</td><td><span class="ex">They decided. She applied. The plan succeeded.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>linking verb + ___</td><td>adjective or noun complement</td><td><span class="ex">The plan was successful. Jo became an applicant.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>___ + noun</td><td>adjective</td><td><span class="ex">a decisive response · reliable transport · productive work</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>action verb + ___</td><td>often adverb</td><td><span class="ex">responded decisively · worked productively · explained clearly</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>preposition + ___</td><td>noun, pronoun, or -ing form</td><td><span class="ex">after the decision · by applying · for employment</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Position is evidence, not an automatic answer. After <span class="ex">be</span>, both a noun and an adjective can be possible: <span class="ex">Mina is an applicant</span> identifies her role, while <span class="ex">Mina is successful</span> describes her result. Use the sentence meaning together with the grammatical frame.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the possessive, tense, linking verb, noun modifier, and action modifier to select decision, apply, successful, reliable, and clearly.">
    <h3>Choose the family member required by job and meaning</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Word-family slot choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="decision">decision</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="apply">apply</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="successful">successful</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reliable">reliable</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="clearly">clearly</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="successfully application">successfully application</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The board explained its final <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="decision" data-hint="The possessive its and adjective final introduce a noun naming what the board decided." data-fix="The board explained its final decision.">choose</button> in writing.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Applicants must <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="apply" data-hint="Must takes the base verb form." data-fix="Applicants must apply by Friday.">choose</button> by Friday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The application met every stated requirement, so it was <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="successful" data-hint="The linking verb was connects the application to an adjective describing its result." data-fix="The application was successful.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The evening group needs <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reliable" data-hint="The blank directly describes the noun transport, so use the adjective from the rely family." data-fix="The evening group needs reliable transport.">choose</button> transport that arrives at the scheduled time.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The presenter organized the evidence and explained the conclusion <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="clearly" data-hint="Use an adverb to modify how the presenter explained the conclusion." data-fix="The presenter explained the conclusion clearly.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Learn family meaning, spelling, and constraints together</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>family</th><th>members and distinct jobs</th><th>important boundary</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>decide</td><td><span class="ex">decide · decision · decisive · decisively</span></td><td><span class="ex">decisive</span> can mean settling an issue or showing clear, effective decision</td></tr>
      <tr><td>apply</td><td><span class="ex">apply · application · applicant · applicable</span></td><td><span class="ex">application</span> can be a request, form, practical use, or act of applying</td></tr>
      <tr><td>employ</td><td><span class="ex">employ · employer · employee · employment · unemployed</span></td><td><span class="ex">employer</span> provides work; <span class="ex">employee</span> works for the employer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>produce</td><td><span class="ex">produce · product · production · productive · productivity</span></td><td><span class="ex">production</span> is a process or amount produced; <span class="ex">productivity</span> relates output to time or resources</td></tr>
      <tr><td>succeed</td><td><span class="ex">succeed · success · successful · successfully</span></td><td><span class="ex">succeed in + noun/-ing</span>; <span class="ex">successful in/at</span> depends on the following meaning</td></tr>
      <tr><td>rely</td><td><span class="ex">rely · reliability · reliable · reliably · unreliable</span></td><td>the spelling is <span class="ex">reliable</span>, not <span class="ex">relyable</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Suffixes can provide clues: <span class="ex">-tion, -ment, -ity</span> often form nouns; <span class="ex">-ive, -al, -ful</span> often form adjectives; <span class="ex">-ly</span> often forms adverbs. They are not mechanical guarantees. <span class="ex">Friendly, lively, likely</span>, and <span class="ex">lonely</span> are adjectives. Spelling and meaning may change, as in <span class="ex">apply → application</span>, <span class="ex">decide → decision</span>, and <span class="ex">succeed → success</span>. Store the real family member, not an invented form.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished applicant from application, employer from employee, production from productivity, succeeded in from a noun substitution, and reliable from an invented spelling.">
    <h3>Choose the family meaning established by the context</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Word-family meaning choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="applicant">applicant</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="employer">employer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="productivity">productivity</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="succeeded in meeting">succeeded in meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reliable">reliable</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="relyable">relyable</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Jordan submitted the request and is the person seeking the grant. Jordan is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="applicant" data-hint="Use the person noun, not application, which names the request or process." data-fix="Jordan is the applicant.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Harbor Design hires and pays twelve employees. In this relationship, Harbor Design is the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="employer" data-hint="The -er form names the organization that provides the employment." data-fix="Harbor Design is the employer.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The team produced the same 40 accurate reports in 30 hours instead of 50 hours. Its output per hour, or <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="productivity" data-hint="The context compares output with the time resource used, not merely the production process." data-fix="The team’s productivity increased.">choose</button>, increased.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The goal was to satisfy all five published requirements, and the final file satisfied all five. The team <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="succeeded in meeting" data-hint="Succeed takes in before a noun or -ing form; meeting names fulfillment of the requirements." data-fix="The team succeeded in meeting all five requirements.">choose</button> all five requirements.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The bus arrived at the scheduled time on 29 of 30 observed days. The report describes the service as <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reliable" data-hint="Use the established adjective spelling from the rely family." data-fix="The report describes the service as reliable.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Allow natural alternatives while controlling register and meaning</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Collocations have preferences, not identical rules in every setting. <span class="ex">Do research</span> is neutral and common; <span class="ex">conduct research</span> is more formal and common in academic or professional method descriptions. <span class="ex">Strongly recommend</span> and <span class="ex">highly recommend</span> are both natural. <span class="ex">Reach an agreement</span> and <span class="ex">come to an agreement</span> are both standard. The important learner skill is choosing a natural form that preserves the intended stance.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>choice</th><th>typical use</th><th>do not overclaim</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">do / conduct research</span></td><td>neutral activity / more formal method or institutional process</td><td>neither phrase automatically proves quality</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">strongly / highly recommend</span></td><td>forceful stance / very favorable degree; substantial overlap</td><td>both are natural current U.S. English</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">big problem / major issue</span></td><td>common conversation / more formal or institutional framing</td><td>big problem is not grammatically inferior</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">totally / strongly agree</span></td><td>especially conversational emphasis / formal discussion or survey scale</td><td>actual tone depends on the whole sentence and situation</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">reach / come to an agreement</span></td><td>concise result / process leading to a shared result</td><td>both can report the same final outcome</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You preserved the neutral-formal research contrast, accepted both recommendation patterns, recognized two agreement patterns, distinguished conversational and formal problem language, and matched agreement intensity to setting.">
    <h3>Choose the accurate statement about variation</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Collocation variation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="do research is neutral and conduct research is more formal">do research is neutral and conduct research is more formal</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="strongly recommend and highly recommend are both natural">strongly recommend and highly recommend are both natural</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reach an agreement and come to an agreement are both standard">reach an agreement and come to an agreement are both standard</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="big problem is common in conversation while major issue is more formal">big problem is common in conversation while major issue is more formal</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="totally agree is especially conversational while strongly agree fits formal discussion or a survey scale">totally agree is especially conversational while strongly agree fits formal discussion or a survey scale</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="only one phrase can ever be grammatical in every setting">only one phrase can ever be grammatical in every setting</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Which comparison accurately describes a neutral conversation and a formal methods section? <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="do research is neutral and conduct research is more formal" data-hint="Both are natural; conduct commonly carries the more formal institutional tone." data-fix="Do research is neutral, and conduct research is more formal.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Which statement avoids inventing a false rule about recommendations? <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="strongly recommend and highly recommend are both natural" data-hint="Current U.S. English uses both adverbs with recommend, with substantial overlap." data-fix="Strongly recommend and highly recommend are both natural.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Which statement recognizes two established ways to report a shared result? <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="reach an agreement and come to an agreement are both standard" data-hint="One is concise and one can highlight the process, but both are standard." data-fix="Reach an agreement and come to an agreement are both standard.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Which statement accurately compares likely register without calling conversation incorrect? <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="big problem is common in conversation while major issue is more formal" data-hint="Big problem is natural; major issue often sounds more institutional or formal." data-fix="Big problem is common in conversation, while major issue is more formal.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Which statement fits a friendly conversation versus a formal agreement scale? <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="totally agree is especially conversational while strongly agree fits formal discussion or a survey scale" data-hint="Match the intensifier to the supplied channel and level of formality." data-fix="Totally agree is especially conversational, while strongly agree fits formal discussion or a survey scale.">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Sound natural: learn stress and sound with each family member</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Spelling does not preserve one stress pattern across a family. The stress is on the second syllable in <span class="ex">deCIDE</span> /dɪˈsaɪd/, <span class="ex">deCIsion</span> /dɪˈsɪʒən/, and <span class="ex">deCIsive</span> /dɪˈsaɪsɪv/, but the vowel and final consonant pattern change. Say the whole member, not a root plus an imagined ending.</p>
  <p>Stress moves across <span class="ex">apPLY</span> /əˈplaɪ/, <span class="ex">appliCAtion</span> /ˌæpləˈkeɪʃən/, and <span class="ex">APplicant</span> /ˈæplɪkənt/. It also moves across <span class="ex">PHOtograph</span> /ˈfoʊtəˌɡræf/, <span class="ex">phoTOGraphy</span> /fəˈtɑɡrəfi/, and <span class="ex">photoGRAPHic</span> /ˌfoʊtəˈɡræfɪk/.</p>
  <p>Grammatical job can change stress in the same spelling: <span class="ex">make PROgress</span> uses the noun /ˈprɑɡrɛs/, while <span class="ex">the project will proGRESS</span> uses the verb /prəˈɡrɛs/. In connected chunks, link the smaller words: <span class="ex">make_an_effort</span>, <span class="ex">reach_an_agreement</span>, <span class="ex">responsible_for</span>. Sentence focus can still move the strongest stress when a speaker corrects or contrasts information.</p>
  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read collocation retrieval, noun-verb progress stress, decide-family sounds, apply-family stress, register alternatives, and linked-chunk listening check">
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard the noun-verb progress shift, decide-family sound changes, application stress, legitimate recommendation alternatives, and linked agreement chunk.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening: recover the family and partnership</h3>
      <p class="note-line">The tutor reads each line once naturally and once with the target stress emphasized. The learner answers before seeing the written line again.</p>
      <div class="quiz-list">
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The first is the noun after make; the second is the verb after will." data-hint="Listen for PRO-gress in the noun phrase and pro-GRESS in the verb phrase." data-fix="Make PROgress uses the noun; will proGRESS uses the verb."><p class="quiz-question">1. Tutor line: “The team made PROgress, and the project will proGRESS next month.” What does the stress shift mark?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The first is the noun after make; the second is the verb after will.">The first is the noun after make; the second is the verb after will.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both uses are adverbs modifying month.">Both uses are adverbs modifying month.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The stress changes progress into the opposite meaning.">The stress changes progress into the opposite meaning.</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="All three have second-syllable stress, while their vowels and consonants are not identical." data-hint="Hear de-CIDE, de-CI-sion, and de-CI-sive, then compare the sounds after the stressed syllable." data-fix="Decide, decision, and decisive all have second-syllable stress, with sound changes inside the family."><p class="quiz-question">2. Tutor line: “They deCIDE, explain the deCIsion, and take deCIsive action.” Which description is accurate?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="All three have second-syllable stress, while their vowels and consonants are not identical.">All three have second-syllable stress, while their vowels and consonants are not identical.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Decide has first-syllable stress and every member keeps identical sounds.">Decide has first-syllable stress and every member keeps identical sounds.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Decision is an adverb because the stress is on de.">Decision is an adverb because the stress is on de.</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Application is the submitted request, and successful describes its result." data-hint="Hear appli-CA-tion as the noun and suc-CESS-ful as the adjective after was." data-fix="Application names the request; successful describes the result."><p class="quiz-question">3. Tutor line: “Her appliCAtion was sucCESSful.” What jobs do the two stressed family members perform?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Application is the submitted request, and successful describes its result.">Application is the submitted request, and successful describes its result.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Application describes how she acted, and successful is the action verb.">Application describes how she acted, and successful is the action verb.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both words name the person who reviewed the request.">Both words name the person who reviewed the request.</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Both strongly recommend and highly recommend are natural; the speakers chose different emphasis." data-hint="The lesson accepts both established adverb-verb combinations." data-fix="Strongly recommend and highly recommend are both natural, with overlapping but potentially different emphasis."><p class="quiz-question">4. Tutor lines: “I STRONGLY recommend the course.” “I HIGHLY recommend the course.” What does the pair demonstrate?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both strongly recommend and highly recommend are natural; the speakers chose different emphasis.">Both strongly recommend and highly recommend are natural; the speakers chose different emphasis.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Only strongly recommend can ever be grammatical.">Only strongly recommend can ever be grammatical.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Highly turns recommend into a noun.">Highly turns recommend into a noun.</button></div></div>
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The speaker links reach, an, and agreement as one retrievable chunk." data-hint="Listen across the word boundaries rather than adding a pause before an." data-fix="Reach an agreement is linked as one fluent lexical chunk."><p class="quiz-question">5. Tutor line: “We need to reach_an_agreement by Friday.” What connected-speech feature supports retrieval?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The speaker links reach, an, and agreement as one retrievable chunk.">The speaker links reach, an, and agreement as one retrievable chunk.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The speaker deletes agreement and says only reach.">The speaker deletes agreement and says only reach.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Each sound requires a long pause before the next word.">Each sound requires a long pause before the next word.</button></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Read a project file as a network of lexical decisions</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Westbridge Skills Exchange pilot</h3>
    <p><b>Need and research:</b> The organizers conducted a four-week needs survey with 80 adult residents. Forty-six respondents requested evening job-search workshops, and 29 identified unreliable evening transport as a major barrier.</p>
    <p><b>Preparation:</b> The team made steady progress on a workshop guide, reached an agreement with the public library, and met the June 15 grant deadline. The grant application was successful and provided funding for six workshops.</p>
    <p><b>Delivery:</b> Thirty-eight participants attended at least one workshop, and 31 attended four or more. Facilitators provided practical interview experience through role plays. Twenty-eight of the 31 regular participants rated the mock interviews useful.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence limit:</b> The pilot did not collect later employment outcomes, compare a control group, or test whether the workshops caused a change. The attendance and feedback are encouraging, but they do not establish long-term effectiveness.</p>
    <p><b>Remaining issue:</b> Nine participants reported missing at least one session because of evening transportation. The team is seriously considering a remote option, but reliable internet access and privacy remain important concerns.</p>
    <p><b>Next step:</b> The team recommends another six-workshop pilot with attendance, access, participant feedback, and voluntary three-month follow-up measures. It will compare the in-person and remote options before making a larger decision.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You recovered the formal research action, four project achievements, the application family, the limited success evidence, and the transport-access recommendation.">
    <h3>Evidence check: retrieve the chunk without inflating the claim</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="The organizers conducted a four-week needs survey with 80 adult residents." data-hint="Use the formal verb from the stated research method and retain its duration and sample." data-fix="The organizers conducted a four-week needs survey with 80 adult residents."><p class="quiz-question">1. Which sentence accurately summarizes the initial research?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The organizers conducted a four-week needs survey with 80 adult residents.">The organizers conducted a four-week needs survey with 80 adult residents.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The organizers made a research that proved every resident had the same need.">The organizers made a research that proved every resident had the same need.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="No participant or method was recorded.">No participant or method was recorded.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="They made progress, reached an agreement, met the deadline, and secured workshop funding." data-hint="Recover the guide, library, deadline, and successful grant result as four separate achievements." data-fix="They made progress, reached an agreement, met the deadline, and secured workshop funding."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which sequence reports the preparation achievements?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They made progress, reached an agreement, met the deadline, and secured workshop funding.">They made progress, reached an agreement, met the deadline, and secured workshop funding.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They did progress, arrived to an agreement, lost every deadline, and refused the grant.">They did progress, arrived to an agreement, lost every deadline, and refused the grant.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="They measured long-term employment before the pilot began.">They measured long-term employment before the pilot began.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Application names the request; successful describes its result." data-hint="Use the noun after grant and the adjective after was." data-fix="Application names the request, and successful describes its result."><p class="quiz-question">3. What jobs do <span class="ex">application</span> and <span class="ex">successful</span> perform in the preparation paragraph?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Application names the request; successful describes its result.">Application names the request; successful describes its result.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Application is the applicant, and successful is the grant action.">Application is the applicant, and successful is the grant action.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Both words are prepositions introducing June 15.">Both words are prepositions introducing June 15.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Attendance and feedback are encouraging, but the pilot did not establish long-term effectiveness or causation." data-hint="Keep the positive recorded evidence and all three study limits together." data-fix="Attendance and feedback are encouraging, but the pilot did not establish long-term effectiveness or causation."><p class="quiz-question">4. Which conclusion matches the evidence collected?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Attendance and feedback are encouraging, but the pilot did not establish long-term effectiveness or causation.">Attendance and feedback are encouraging, but the pilot did not establish long-term effectiveness or causation.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="The feedback proves every participant found a job because of the workshops.">The feedback proves every participant found a job because of the workshops.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Because no employment outcome was measured, attendance and feedback did not occur.">Because no employment outcome was measured, attendance and feedback did not occur.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Test another pilot while comparing attendance, access, feedback, follow-up, and the in-person and remote options." data-hint="Recover the proposed action, four evidence areas, and option comparison." data-fix="Test another pilot while comparing attendance, access, feedback, follow-up, and the in-person and remote options."><p class="quiz-question">5. Which recommendation preserves the remaining transport and access questions?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Test another pilot while comparing attendance, access, feedback, follow-up, and the in-person and remote options.">Test another pilot while comparing attendance, access, feedback, follow-up, and the in-person and remote options.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Declare the remote option perfect before checking internet access or privacy.">Declare the remote option perfect before checking internet access or privacy.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Ignore the nine transportation reports and collect no later evidence.">Ignore the nine transportation reports and collect no later evidence.</button></div></div>
